//! The network capability (Phase 12, ADR 0183): the right to use the
//! network, as a range of its ports.
//!
//! A network capability designates a range of ports, the 65,536 of the
//! whole network for the first, which the kernel gives Spark when it
//! starts. Its send right lets its holder connect out, to any address and
//! port beyond the networks this machine is on, from a port the kernel
//! picks: what the permission network.outbound grants. Its local right
//! reaches the machines on those networks, and their broadcast and
//! multicast groups, which a socket may join (ADR 0337): what
//! network.local grants; a service's network.outbound grants both.
//! Either reaches this machine's own addresses. Its receive right lets
//! its holder bind a socket to a port in its range, and listen there for
//! the connections that come: network.listen, for those ports.
//! `restrict` derives one for fewer ports, or fewer rights, which Spark
//! gives each service as its manifest asks.
//!
//! Sockets are made from a network capability (`socket`), and are files
//! (radix-file(2)) with the socket methods: `bind_address`,
//! `connect_address`, `send_to` and `receive_from` take the slot of a
//! network capability, which must allow what they do, and `listen`
//! needs a socket bound with the receive right for its port. A program
//! finds its network capabilities in radix-startup(2)'s NETWORK_SLOTS
//! slots from SLOT_NETWORK.
//!
//! Its control right, whatever its ports, sets the interfaces up
//! (`configure` and `configure_ipv6`): their addresses and their
//! routers, which ip(8) and netd(8) do (ADR 0190), and how they make
//! their IPv6 addresses (ADR 0331). Spark gives a service one with only
//! that right when its file asks for admin.network. The interfaces are numbered as
//! Linux numbers them: `lo` is 1, and the cards from 2 in the order they
//! were found, `net0` first; `/kernel/class/net` has a directory for
//! each, with its number in `ifindex`.
//!
//! The control right also makes bridges and VLANs (`add_link`), joins
//! interfaces to a bridge (`set_master`) and takes bridges and VLANs
//! away (`remove_link`), as ip(8)'s `link` commands do (ADR 0333). They
//! are numbered after the cards, in the first place free; a place one
//! was taken away from is not used again until the machine restarts.
//!
//! It makes tunnels too, interfaces whose IP packets a program carries,
//! as wgd(8) carries WireGuard's (`add_link`), and opens them for that
//! program (`open_tunnel`, ADR 0339).
//!
//! And it opens packet captures (`open_capture`, ADR 0346): files whose
//! reads take the frames an interface receives and sends, those a
//! classic BPF filter picks, for tcpdump(8) and the rest of libpcap's
//! programs, as Linux's packet sockets and the BSDs' BPF devices give
//! them.

module network;

/// The first port the kernel picks from when a socket needs one of its
/// own, to connect from or when bound to port 0: Linux's and IANA's
/// dynamic ports, 49152 to 65535.
const EPHEMERAL_FIRST: u64 = 49152;

/// The version of IP an address is of.
enum AddressFamily: u32 {
    /// IPv4, POSIX's AF_INET.
    ipv4 = 4;
    /// IPv6, POSIX's AF_INET6 (since Phase 12c, ADR 0332).
    ipv6 = 6;
}

/// What a socket carries.
enum SocketType: u32 {
    /// A stream of bytes, in order, over a connection: TCP, POSIX's
    /// SOCK_STREAM.
    stream = 1;
    /// Datagrams, each on its own, which may be lost: UDP, POSIX's
    /// SOCK_DGRAM.
    datagram = 2;
    /// ICMP's or ICMPv6's echo requests, and the replies to them: POSIX's
    /// SOCK_DGRAM with IPPROTO_ICMP or IPPROTO_ICMPV6, as Linux's ping
    /// sockets (ADR 0336). A datagram sent is an echo request, its
    /// header's identifier and checksum the kernel's; one received is a
    /// reply to the socket's own, its header and all. Bound or not, it
    /// has an identifier of its own, as a datagram socket has a port, and
    /// the Address's port is it. (Since version 5, Phase 12c.3.)
    ping = 3;
}

/// An address and port: where a socket is bound or connected, or where a
/// datagram goes or came from, as POSIX's sockaddr_in and sockaddr_in6.
/// An IPv6 socket's are IPv6 addresses, those of IPv4 machines as
/// IPv4-mapped ones, ::ffff:10.0.2.2.
struct Address {
    /// Which version of IP `ip` is of: an AddressFamily, the socket's.
    family: u32;
    /// The port, as a number: 0 for any, when binding.
    port: u16;
    /// Zero.
    reserved: u16;
    /// The address, in network byte order: an IPv4 address in the first
    /// 4 bytes, the rest zero. All zero is any address, when binding.
    ip: [u8; 16];
    /// For a link-local IPv6 address (fe80::/10), the interface it is on,
    /// by its number, as sockaddr_in6's sin6_scope_id: 0 for whichever
    /// has IPv6 first, or the one the socket is bound to. Zero otherwise.
    /// (Since version 3.)
    scope: u32;
}

/// An ICMP or ICMPv6 error a datagram or ping socket heard about what it
/// sent, as radix-file(2)'s `receive_error` gives it: what Linux's
/// sock_extended_err and its offender say, and where the packet went
/// (ADR 0345). (Since Phase 12.)
struct SocketError {
    /// What it came as, Linux's ee_origin: 2 for ICMP, 3 for ICMPv6.
    origin: u8;
    /// The ICMP or ICMPv6 message's type.
    kind: u8;
    /// Its code.
    code: u8;
    /// Zero.
    reserved: u8;
    /// What it says besides, Linux's ee_info: the next hop's MTU of a
    /// "fragmentation needed" or "packet too big", the pointer of a
    /// "parameter problem"; 0 otherwise.
    info: u32;
    /// The error the socket's next send or receive fails with for it, as
    /// a status word: host_unreachable for a router's "time exceeded",
    /// refused for a machine's "port unreachable", and so on.
    error: u64;
    /// Who sent it: a router on the packet's way, or the machine it went
    /// to; an Address of the socket's family, its port 0.
    offender: Address;
    /// Where the packet went: the address and port it was sent to, a
    /// ping socket's port its echo identifier's 0.
    destination: Address;
}

/// The most IPv4 addresses `configure` gives an interface.
const ADDRESSES_MAX: u64 = 4;

/// An IPv4 address of an interface's, and its network: what `configure`
/// takes, as `ip address add 10.0.2.15/24` writes it.
struct InterfaceAddress {
    /// The address, in network byte order.
    ip: [u8; 4];
    /// The length of its network's prefix, 0 to 32.
    prefix: u8;
    /// Zero.
    reserved: [u8; 3];
}

/// The most IPv6 addresses `configure_ipv6` gives an interface besides
/// those it makes itself.
const ADDRESSES_IPV6_MAX: u64 = 4;

/// An IPv6 address of an interface's, and its network's prefix length:
/// what `configure_ipv6` takes, as `ip address add 2001:db8::5/64` writes
/// it.
struct InterfaceAddressIpv6 {
    /// The address, in network byte order.
    ip: [u8; 16];
    /// The length of its network's prefix, 1 to 128.
    prefix: u8;
    /// Zero.
    reserved: [u8; 7];
}

/// How an interface makes its IPv6 addresses (ADR 0331).
flags Ipv6Flags: u32 {
    /// It has IPv6: a link-local address, made from `secret`, and those
    /// it is given. Without it, it has none, and takes no IPv6 packets.
    enabled = 0;
    /// It makes stable addresses from the prefixes routers advertise
    /// (RFC 4862 and RFC 7217), and takes their routes and DNS servers.
    autoconf = 1;
    /// It makes temporary addresses from them too (RFC 8981), and sends
    /// from those.
    temporary = 2;
    /// Only the addresses it was given and its router change: how it
    /// makes the others, and those it made, stay, and the other flags and
    /// the secret are not looked at. What ip(8) changes.
    given_only = 3;
}

/// What `configure_ipv6` sets besides the addresses.
struct Ipv6Settings {
    /// Ipv6Flags.
    flags: u32;
    /// Zero.
    reserved: u32;
    /// The key its stable addresses are made with: the same at each boot,
    /// so that they are, and secret, so that nobody else can tell them
    /// apart from random ones. netd(8) keeps the machine's.
    secret: [u8; 16];
}

/// What `add_link` makes (since version 4, ADR 0333).
enum LinkKind: u32 {
    /// A bridge: the interfaces made its ports are joined into one
    /// network, and it has addresses as a card does, which its ports do
    /// not while they are its.
    bridge = 1;
    /// A VLAN on another interface, IEEE 802.1Q's: what it sends goes out
    /// of that interface tagged with its VLAN number, and the frames that
    /// interface receives tagged with it come to it.
    vlan = 2;
    /// A tunnel: IP packets alone, with no Ethernet header, MAC address
    /// or neighbours, at most 1420 bytes each, which go to and come from
    /// the program that opens it (`open_tunnel`). Its link is down while
    /// no program has it open. (Since version 6, ADR 0339.)
    tunnel = 3;
}

/// The most bytes of each frame a packet capture keeps: libpcap's
/// MAXIMUM_SNAPLEN, tcpdump(8)'s default snap length.
const CAPTURE_SNAP_MAX: u64 = 262144;
/// The fewest bytes of frames not yet read a packet capture keeps.
const CAPTURE_BUFFER_MIN: u64 = 65536;
/// The most.
const CAPTURE_BUFFER_MAX: u64 = 67108864;
/// What it keeps when `open_capture` is given 0: libpcap's default on
/// Linux, 2 MiB.
const CAPTURE_BUFFER_DEFAULT: u64 = 2097152;
/// The most packet captures open at once, between all programs.
const CAPTURES_MAX: u64 = 16;

/// radix-file(2)'s `control` request that gives a packet capture's file
/// a filter: `buffer` holds `size` / 8 classic BPF instructions, 1 to
/// 4096 of them, laid out as Linux's struct sock_filter and libpcap's
/// struct bpf_insn, which the kernel runs on each frame from then on,
/// keeping only those it returns more than 0 for, cut to that many bytes
/// (ADR 0346); with `size` 0 it takes the filter away, and every frame is
/// kept. Fails with invalid_argument for a program the kernel does not
/// run: an instruction not classic BPF's, a jump past the end, a memory
/// word past the sixteen, a division by a constant 0, or a last
/// instruction that does not return. The frames waiting are thrown away,
/// as they were not filtered, and the counts start again, as the BSDs'
/// BIOCSETF does. `argument` is not used.
const CAPTURE_FILTER: u32 = 0x52430001;
/// radix-file(2)'s `control` request that writes a packet capture's
/// CaptureCounts into `buffer`, whose `size` is 16. `argument` is not
/// used.
const CAPTURE_COUNTS: u32 = 0x52430002;

/// A frame's link-layer, as pcap's link types number them: an Ethernet
/// frame, header and all (LINKTYPE_ETHERNET).
const CAPTURE_LINK_ETHERNET: u64 = 1;
/// A frame that is an IP packet alone, IPv4 or IPv6 by its first byte's
/// version: a tunnel's (LINKTYPE_RAW).
const CAPTURE_LINK_RAW: u64 = 101;

/// A frame a packet capture saw, as a read of its file gives it: this,
/// then `captured` bytes of the frame, then zeros to a multiple of 8
/// bytes, where the next one starts.
struct CaptureHeader {
    /// When it was seen: nanoseconds since 1970, by the time of day, or
    /// since the machine started while that is not known.
    time: u64;
    /// The frame's length.
    length: u32;
    /// The bytes of it that follow: as many as the snap length and the
    /// filter keep, at most `length`.
    captured: u32;
    /// The interface it was seen on, by its number.
    interface: u32;
    /// Its link-layer: CAPTURE_LINK_ETHERNET or CAPTURE_LINK_RAW.
    link: u16;
    /// 0 when the interface received it, 1 when it sent it.
    outgoing: u8;
    /// Zero.
    reserved: u8;
}

/// What a packet capture counted since it opened, or since its filter
/// was last set: what `control`'s CAPTURE_COUNTS writes.
struct CaptureCounts {
    /// The frames it kept: those its filter took, or all without one.
    received: u64;
    /// Of those, the frames lost as its file was full.
    dropped: u64;
}

/// A range of the network's ports, and the rights to connect out, to
/// listen on them, and to set the interfaces up.
interface network = 0x000f version 7 {
    kind = network;
    /// Makes a socket of `family` and `kind` in slot `to`, with `flags`:
    /// one that is neither bound nor connected (POSIX's `socket`). Needs
    /// the send, the local or the receive right; a ping socket, the send
    /// or the local right.
    method socket = 1 (to: slot, family: AddressFamily, kind: SocketType, flags: PipeFlags) -> ()
        raises [no_such_slot, empty_slot, wrong_kind, missing_rights, occupied, no_memory,
                not_supported];
    /// Puts in slot `to` a network capability derived from this one, for
    /// the `count` ports from `first`, which must be among its own, and
    /// with `rights`, of its own. Needs the copy right.
    method restrict = 2 (to: slot, first: u32, count: u32, rights: Rights) -> ()
        raises [no_such_slot, empty_slot, wrong_kind, missing_rights, occupied, out_of_range,
                too_deep, revoking, no_memory];
    /// The capability's range of ports: the first, and how many.
    method ports = 3 () -> (first: u32, count: u32)
        raises [no_such_slot, empty_slot, wrong_kind];
    /// Gives the interface numbered `interface` the IPv4 addresses in
    /// `addresses`, InterfaceAddress structures, at most ADDRESSES_MAX
    /// of them, in place of those it had, the first its own for the
    /// packets it sends; and `router`, an address in network byte order,
    /// as its default route, or none if `router` is empty. No addresses
    /// and no router leave it with neither, as a DHCP client starts.
    /// Each address is a unicast one; so is the router. Fails with
    /// no_device for an interface that is not there, and not_supported
    /// for `lo`, whose addresses are its own and the cards'. Needs the
    /// control right. (Since version 2.)
    method configure = 4 (interface: u32, addresses: in bytes, router: in bytes) -> ()
        raises [no_such_slot, empty_slot, wrong_kind, missing_rights, invalid_argument,
                bad_address, no_device, not_supported];
    /// Sets how the interface numbered `interface` makes its IPv6
    /// addresses, `settings`, an Ipv6Settings structure, and gives it the
    /// IPv6 addresses in `addresses`, InterfaceAddressIpv6 structures, at
    /// most ADDRESSES_IPV6_MAX, in place of those it was given before;
    /// and `router`, 16 bytes, as its default route while no router
    /// advertises one, or none if `router` is empty. Each address is
    /// tested first, as those it makes are, and given up if another
    /// machine has it. The addresses it made are made again, unless the
    /// settings are given_only, which fail with not_supported while the
    /// interface's IPv6 is off. Fails as `configure` does besides. Needs
    /// the control right. (Since version 3.)
    method configure_ipv6 = 5 (interface: u32, settings: in bytes, addresses: in bytes,
                               router: in bytes) -> ()
        raises [no_such_slot, empty_slot, wrong_kind, missing_rights, invalid_argument,
                bad_address, no_device, not_supported];
    /// Makes an interface of `kind` named `name`, 1 to 15 letters,
    /// digits, `.`, `-` and `_`: a bridge, a tunnel, or a VLAN numbered
    /// `vlan`, 1 to 4094, on the interface numbered `parent`; both are
    /// ignored for a bridge or a tunnel. Its number. Fails with occupied
    /// if an interface has the name or the parent has a VLAN with that
    /// number, invalid_argument for a name or number not allowed,
    /// no_device for a parent that is not there, not_supported for `lo`
    /// or a tunnel as a parent, and no_room once there are sixteen
    /// interfaces besides `lo`. Needs the control right. (Since version
    /// 4.)
    method add_link = 6 (kind: LinkKind, parent: u32, vlan: u32, name: in bytes)
                        -> (interface: u32)
        raises [no_such_slot, empty_slot, wrong_kind, missing_rights, invalid_argument,
                bad_address, occupied, no_device, not_supported, no_room, no_memory];
    /// Makes the interface numbered `interface` a port of the bridge
    /// numbered `bridge`, or of no bridge for 0. A port's addresses are
    /// taken away, and its frames go through the bridge; a bridge has its
    /// first port's MAC address. Fails with no_device for an interface or
    /// bridge that is not there, and not_supported for `lo`, a bridge or
    /// a tunnel as a port. Needs the control right. (Since version 4.)
    method set_master = 7 (interface: u32, bridge: u32) -> ()
        raises [no_such_slot, empty_slot, wrong_kind, missing_rights, no_device,
                not_supported, no_memory];
    /// Takes the bridge, VLAN or tunnel numbered `interface` away, and
    /// the VLANs on it; a bridge's ports are left as interfaces of their
    /// own, without addresses, and a tunnel's file fails as no_device from
    /// then on. Fails with no_device for one that is not there, and
    /// not_supported for `lo` or a card. Needs the control right. (Since
    /// version 4.)
    method remove_link = 8 (interface: u32) -> ()
        raises [no_such_slot, empty_slot, wrong_kind, missing_rights, no_device,
                not_supported, no_memory];
    /// Puts in slot `to`, with `flags`, the file of the tunnel numbered
    /// `interface`, the other end of its link: a read takes the next IP
    /// packet its stack sent, waiting for one unless the file is
    /// non-blocking, cut to the buffer if it is longer; a write gives its
    /// stack one packet, IPv4 or IPv6 by its first byte's version, of 1 to
    /// 1518 bytes, else fails with invalid_argument, and never waits: one
    /// that finds the stack's queue full is lost. A poll finds the file
    /// readable while a packet waits. While the file is open, the
    /// tunnel's link is up; when its last description goes, the link is
    /// down, and what its stack sends is lost until it is opened again.
    /// Fails with no_device for an interface that is not a tunnel, busy
    /// while another file has it open, and occupied if the slot is not
    /// empty. Needs the control right. (Since version 6, ADR 0339.)
    method open_tunnel = 9 (to: slot, interface: u32, flags: PipeFlags) -> ()
        raises [no_such_slot, empty_slot, wrong_kind, missing_rights, no_device, busy,
                occupied, no_memory];
    /// Puts in slot `to`, with `flags`, a packet capture of the interface
    /// numbered `interface`, or of every interface for 0, those added
    /// later among them (ADR 0346): a file whose reads take the frames
    /// the interface receives and sends from then on, each a
    /// CaptureHeader and its first `snap` bytes, 1 to CAPTURE_SNAP_MAX,
    /// at most. A frame received is seen before anything takes it, the
    /// firewall and the VLANs above the interface among them; a loopback
    /// packet once, as it is received, with an Ethernet header of zero
    /// addresses before it, as Linux's `lo` gives it, and a tunnel's as
    /// the IP packet it is. A read takes as many whole frames as fit in
    /// its buffer, waiting for one unless the file is non-blocking, and
    /// cuts the first to the buffer if it alone does not fit. The file
    /// keeps `buffer` bytes of frames not yet read, CAPTURE_BUFFER_MIN to
    /// CAPTURE_BUFFER_MAX, or CAPTURE_BUFFER_DEFAULT for 0; a frame that
    /// finds no room is lost, and counted. A poll finds the file readable
    /// while a frame waits. Its `control` takes CAPTURE_FILTER and
    /// CAPTURE_COUNTS; a write fails with not_supported. Once the
    /// interface is taken away, a read finds the frames that wait, then
    /// fails with no_device, and a poll finds the file in error. Fails
    /// with no_device for an interface that is not there,
    /// invalid_argument for a snap length or buffer out of range, busy
    /// while CAPTURES_MAX are open, as the BSDs' BPF devices once were,
    /// and occupied if the slot is not empty. Needs the control right.
    /// (Since version 7, ADR 0346.)
    method open_capture = 10 (to: slot, interface: u32, snap: u32, buffer: u32, flags: PipeFlags)
                             -> ()
        raises [no_such_slot, empty_slot, wrong_kind, missing_rights, invalid_argument,
                no_device, busy, occupied, no_memory];
}
